{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF #16 — e369 + parity epic 3e73. READ THIS, THEN #15, THEN #14. ===\n\nWORKTREE /Users/joeoakhart/rebar-jira-family. ALWAYS: export PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\"\nCLOSES: run from a worktree whose HEAD CONTAINS the commits (/Users/joeoakhart/agent-5200-diag is\nkept for this). `--ref` does NOT satisfy the file_impact precheck. And check gerrit/main, NOT\norigin/main, when verifying a submit — the GitHub mirror lags and made landed work look absent.\nDO NOT trust the board below; read the tickets.\n\n*** THE OPERATOR MADE A SCOPE RULING. IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT THING IN THIS HANDOFF. ***\nJ11's seven new mutation cells found THREE more DC defects. Asked whether they gate e369, the\noperator chose the STRICTEST reading: **ALL THREE ARE NOW e369 CHILDREN and must be fixed before the\nepic closes.** They were reparented accordingly. So e369 went 31/32 -> **31/35**. Do not \"simplify\"\nthis back to follow-ups; it is a deliberate call for maximum defect-free confidence.\n\n--- BOARD ---\ne369 31/35. OPEN: 5200 (in_progress), 751e (in_progress), 2b16 (open), 88d9 (open).\n3e73 4/6. OPEN: 0fad (in_progress, work MERGED — closeable), 2127 (open, LEAVE IT — see below).\n  ALL FOUR P1 CHILDREN OF 3e73 ARE CLOSED: cedc, 79d5, 2944, bd9e. Half the stop condition is met.\n\n--- THE THREE NEW DEFECTS, AND THE PATTERN THEY COMPLETE ---\nAll three were found by cells that assert the CORRECT contract, so **those cells are EXPECTED RED\nuntil the fixes land. DO NOT invert them to make CI green** — that instruction is on 5200 too.\n * [rebar:751e-06f1-bb0b-464c] DC outbound clear-assignee never unassigns. `outbound_differ.py:504-505`\n   resolves an empty assignee to `\"\"`; DC forwards it verbatim to `assign_issue`\n   (`transport.py:281-303`, no empty branch); pycontribs 3.10.5 treats only None/-1/\"-1\" as\n   Unassigned. CLAIMED, agent fixing. Cloud already has the fix at `acli.py:342-345,357-359` (bug 85a1).\n * [rebar:2b16-9be0-a8f5-41d9] inbound link REMOVAL never mirrored — `_diff_links_inbound` is ADD-only\n   by construction (`inbound_differ.py:380,396`) while outbound HAS a removal path. Review running.\n * [rebar:88d9-fe42-e50f-4067] inbound parent CLEAR never emitted — two layers drop it\n   (`fetcher.py:508-511` omits an absent parent; `inbound_differ.py:266-270` refuses parent_id=None),\n   though the apply layer is already ready for it. Queued.\n**THE PATTERN IS NOW FOUR DEEP AND WORTH STATING AS A CLASS: \"Cloud has the translation, DC never got\nits half.\"** d067 (status never transitioned), 8d68 (inbound update never minted an identity), 751e\n(unassign), and 2b16/88d9 as the inbound-mirroring variant. When touching any Cloud/DC shared seam,\nCHECK BOTH SIDES BEFORE ASSUMING PARITY.\n\n--- FILE-CONFLICT MAP (four agents ran in parallel; respect this) ---\n  5200  -> tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py   (MERGED 1251/1252/1253)\n  0fad  -> tests/unit/.../test_acli_graph_pagination_heldout.py (MERGED 1255)\n  049e  -> adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py + settings.py   (claimed, agent running)\n  751e  -> adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py               (claimed, agent running)\n  2b16  -> inbound_differ.py            } **THESE TWO COLLIDE — SERIALIZE THEM**\n  88d9  -> inbound_differ.py + fetcher.py }\n  a3fa  -> dispatch_one.py + dispatch_apply_phases.py          (queued, unsigned)\n\n--- WHAT 0fad PROVED, AND IT CORRECTS AN ASSUMPTION ---\nCloud's whole-project readers (`acli_graph.py` get_parent_map :215, get_comment_map :318,\nget_issuelinks_map :412) page by an OPAQUE CURSOR (`nextPageToken` + `isLast`), NOT by offset\narithmetic. So the DC bug family (advance-by-requested-size, break-on-short-page) **structurally\ncannot occur there** — measured: 250 items at 100/page -> 250 recovered; pages capped at 40 while 100\nrequested -> still 250. Cloud pages correctly, so 0fad's deliverable became a REGRESSION NET, not a\nbug fix. Its net is strong: transplanting the DC bug into Cloud's loop is caught by a dedicated\nshort-page oracle that NO other assertion catches.\nIt did find a DIFFERENT real defect: [rebar:cabc-7a98-d173-4d7c] — a server repeating its cursor token\nmakes those three readers SPIN FOREVER (`fetcher._iter_pages` has exactly this guard at\n`fetcher.py:286-308` -> SilentTruncationError(same-token-twice); the readers do not), and the\nfail-open `except Exception` then turns it into a silently partial map. Its oracle is committed as\n`xfail(strict=True)` citing that bug, so it flips RED the moment the guard lands. Cloud-side, filed,\nnot fixed.\n\n--- 2127: DO NOT START IT ---\nIt diverged over EIGHT review rounds (4->2->3->3->1->3->8 blocking findings), every finding\nreviewer-correct. The cause is structural, not editorial: Cloud has FIVE distinct 429 behaviours, not\nthe two the plan assumed (pooled REST no-retry; ACLI subprocess which DOES retry; a bare `urlopen` in\n`acli_cli_ops.update_priority` that bypasses the retry wrapper; the retrying\n`dispatch_one._call_with_retry`; the explicitly non-retrying `dispatch_apply_phases`) — plus a name\ncollision between two different `update_priority` implementations. It needs someone to settle Cloud's\nretry taxonomy first. It is P2 Cloud-side and explicitly droppable; another editing round burns gate\nrounds for no DC confidence.\n\n--- TRAPS ADDED THIS STRETCH (earlier ones in #15/#14/#13 still hold) ---\n * **SHELL BACKTICKS IN A `rebar comment` ARGUMENT ARE COMMAND-SUBSTITUTED AND SILENTLY DELETE YOUR\n   TEXT.** It cost me three load-bearing sentences on 8d68 — the ones naming what the fix does, which\n   is exactly what a close gate reads — and the comment still landed at a plausible byte length, so\n   neither the exit code nor a length check caught it. Write bodies to a file with a quoted heredoc\n   (`<<'EOF'`), pass `\"$(cat file)\"`, then GREP THE STORED COMMENT for identifiers you expect.\n * **VERIFY A WRITE WITH A PREDICATE THAT MATCHES WHAT YOU ACTUALLY WROTE.** I checked a bd9e comment\n   for a harness run id it never contained, concluded it had failed, and re-posted it four times.\n   Duplicate evidence is harmless; the wasted rounds were not.\n * **THE 800-LOC CAP APPLIES ONLY TO `src/**`.** Both enforcers (`_build-and-test.yml` and\n   `test_module_size_contract.py`) rglob `src_root` only, so a live-suite test module at 1786 lines is\n   ungated. Do NOT \"fix\" it by splitting — splitting a live-suite module risks dropping it from the\n   `_live_jira_ready` all-skip canary, the failure change 1203 had to repair.\n * A ticket with an AC that prescribes a MECHANISM can be wrong about the mechanism. 8d68's close\n   failed on \"cells 2 and 4 invert\" when inverting cell 2 would have asserted the prohibited fix. The\n   honest remedy is amending the criterion WITH the reasoning recorded, not damaging a correct test —\n   but say so loudly, because amending an AC to match what was built is also how a gate gets gamed.\n\n--- IMMEDIATE NEXT ACTIONS ---\n 1. Close 0fad — its change 1255 is MERGED and its evidence is on the ticket.\n 2. Land 049e and 751e (agents running), then close.\n 3. Review -> claim -> fix 2b16, THEN 88d9 (serialize; both touch inbound_differ.py). Then a3fa.\n 4. Re-run the harness. 5200's three new cells (unassign, inbound link removal, inbound parent clear)\n    should flip GREEN as those fixes land; that is what unblocks 5200's close.\n 5. THEN close 5200, then e369 (its own plan review is stale-code and will need re-running first)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785724611152825001,"uuid":"b511d982-4518-4574-8f7b-b3f0cd92922f"}